        "Tax rules vary substantially by country and account type. The source gives US and UK examples; they should not be applied to other jurisdictions without checking local law.\nUnrealised and realised gains\n- An unrealised gain is an increase in an investment’s value while you still hold it.\n- A realised gain occurs when you sell an investment for more than its tax cost or basis, subject to the local rules.\nIn some tax systems, gains are generally recognised on disposal; other rules may apply to funds, distributions, deemed disposals, or particular products. Dividends and interest may also be taxable even if you do not sell the investment.\nHolding period can matter\nThe source describes the US distinction between short-term and long-term capital gains, and notes that the UK uses a different framework. Rates, allowances, thresholds, and eligibility can change. Check current official tax guidance for your jurisdiction and circumstances.\nTax-loss harvesting\nTax-loss harvesting means selling an investment at a loss to realise that loss for possible use against taxable gains, where local rules allow.",
