        "A diversified long-term strategy may be more appropriate for investors who do not want to spend substantial time researching individual companies.\n5. Ignoring fees\nFees reduce the amount of return that remains invested. Compare fund expense ratios, platform charges, spreads, currency costs, and other recurring fees. A low-commission trade can still involve other costs.\n6. Confusing popularity with quality\nA popular company or asset is not automatically a good investment at its current price. Consider business fundamentals, valuation, risk, and portfolio fit.\n7. Failing to diversify\nOwning several investments in the same sector or country may leave the portfolio exposed to one shared risk. Look at underlying holdings and economic drivers.\n8. Investing money needed soon\nMoney required for a near-term expense should not automatically be placed in volatile assets. Match investment risk to the time when the money will be needed."
